WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.360
Upwork, one of the biggest&nbsp;
freelancer platforms in the world.&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.880
Now, it's struggling.
Revenue is flat, their market cap has collapsed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:18.960
86%, from 7.5 billion to barely 1 billion.
Now they're laying off mass amounts of staff.&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.640
And it's entirely Upwork's own fault.
What happens when you keep stacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.280
more and more fees on freelancers?
When you try to squeeze your most loyal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:43.600
users for everything they have.
This is the fall of Upwork.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.600
While remote work may be normal&nbsp;
today, in the 2010s, people were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.520
still figuring out how to make it work.
Freelancers and clients had to learn how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.560
to adapt workplace culture to relationships&nbsp;
built entirely through a screen. How do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:03.360
manage someone not in the office? How do you&nbsp;
know they did work 10 hours and not just 2?&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.120
To solve this, Elance-oDesk,&nbsp;
before it was called Upwork,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:12.080
introduced features like hourly tracking,&nbsp;
activity logs, and periodic screenshots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.360
as many as 6 times an hour.
This sounds annoying… and it was,&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.840
But it did have a purpose.
"I've only ever had one client fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:28.400
me on hours and I won because those screen caps&nbsp;
showed I was actively working on their project."&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.240
"I don't mind a client being able to&nbsp;
see my screen; if I were billing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.640
client without the tool it would be&nbsp;
their project on my screen anyway,"&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.760
But, this doesn't work for everyone,&nbsp;
and there were security concerns.&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:48.400
One thing drew every freelancer in, a flat&nbsp;
fee of 10% for any work done on the platform.&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.760
Pretty great.
But, it wouldn't last.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:58.720
In May 2016, CEO Stéphane Kasriel announced&nbsp;
a new payment system: the sliding fee.&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.520
Instead of the standard 10% commission,&nbsp;
freelancers would now pay 20% on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:09.440
first $500 with a client, 10% from&nbsp;
$500 to $10,000, and 5% above that.&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.640
At first, it didn't sound so bad, and&nbsp;
to some it would even be a discount.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.560
The longer a freelancer worked with&nbsp;
a client, the less they would pay.&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.320
But there was a problem.
Most jobs on Upwork were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.280
short-term gigs: fixing a website, designing&nbsp;
social media posts, writing a script.&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:33.280
As a result, many freelancers never earned enough&nbsp;
from a single client to escape the 20% tier.&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.120
But this was just the beginning.
Upwork also introduced a 2.75% processing fee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:45.120
on client payments, meaning the platform was now&nbsp;
taking a cut from both sides of the transaction.&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:51.200
Sounds small, but that's on the entire job.
Imagine a company spends $100,000 hiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.720
freelancers through Upwork. This processing&nbsp;
fee would add another $2,750 in costs.&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.200
Not to mention the new monthly&nbsp;
subscription fees clients had to pay.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.960
This weird dynamic spilled into&nbsp;
client-freelancer relationships.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.160
Freelancers raised their rates to offset the&nbsp;
fees. Clients pushed back to keep costs down.&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.360
Not because of the value of the&nbsp;
job and the work, but because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:19.920
Upwork's strange fee system playing both sides.
The backlash was immediate but Upwork doubled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.120
down, saying "While it's never easy to make a&nbsp;
big change like this, we decided it was time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.240
to take this step to make Upwork an even better&nbsp;
place for professional freelancers and clients."&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.840
Upwork wasn't budging. And why would they?
Revenue was booming.&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.960
Remote work was becoming more and more&nbsp;
popular, and people were beginning to notice.&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:47.360
So, on October 2, 2018, Upwork went&nbsp;
public. Its IPO was priced at $15 per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:55.920
share for roughly 12.5 million shares. The stock&nbsp;
opened at $23, a 53% jump and closed at $21.18,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.680
up 41% on its first day.
The IPO raised $187 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.480
This was a huge success but also&nbsp;
introduced another long-term problem.&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.640
Upwork now had investors to please and&nbsp;
had to figure out how to extract even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.560
more cash from freelancers.
So came another change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:20.000
arguably worse than the first: Connects.
Connects are, basically, virtual tokens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.680
freelancers must spend to submit proposals.&nbsp;
A free account received about 10 per month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:30.160
barely enough to apply for a single job,&nbsp;
as many listings required between 7 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.280
25 Connects just to click "Apply."
Before this, you could send unlimited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.240
proposals to clients.
Now these Connects are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.960
sold in bundles of 10 for $1.61.
Freelancers were competing against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.720
others who could spend extra Connects&nbsp;
to boost their proposals to the top.&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.840
In other words, you were paying for the privilege&nbsp;
of applying to jobs you might never get.&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.600
And, it gets even worse.
Over the years, the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.400
of Connects required kept climbing.
For freelancers, income was now being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.200
cut from multiple directions: Connects&nbsp;
to bid on jobs, service fees on earnings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:10.400
and withdrawal fees to access their money.
As if that wasn't enough, the 60 free monthly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:16.320
Connects were cut, with new freelancers getting&nbsp;
just 20. Each Connect was priced at $0.15.&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.920
But just like the job fee,&nbsp;
this was framed as a benefit.&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.960
[Jessica Tiwari, VP of product]: "I actually&nbsp;
do not believe that this change will make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.720
harder for new freelancers,"
There is some logic to this.&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.640
This means fewer submissions to clients, and&nbsp;
those clients, according to Upwork, had "expressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.120
feeling overwhelmed by the number of proposals&nbsp;
they received in response to a posted project".&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:41.360
"Upwork said that on average, most freelancers&nbsp;
will spend about $5 or less per month on Connects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.960
but the company didn't provide details&nbsp;
on how it calculated that figure."&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:49.680
[Upwork]: "With paid Connects, we expect&nbsp;
freelancers will submit fewer proposals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.520
increasing your likelihood of winning&nbsp;
projects and making it easier for clients to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.360
identify high-quality talent."
But to many users, it felt like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.480
job hunting itself had become expensive,&nbsp;
and a way for Upwork to extract revenue.&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.080
Imagine if Indeed charged you&nbsp;
for every job you applied to?

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:11.360
Then, things just got better&nbsp;
and better… for Upwork at least.&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.320
The pandemic sent remote work into the mainstream.&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:18.400
With COVID-19, 12% of the U.S.&nbsp;
workforce began freelancing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:24.400
but among Gen Z workers aged 18 to 22, nearly&nbsp;
half entered freelance work during the pandemic.&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.880
At least, according to a study by&nbsp;
Upwork, so take it with a grain of salt.&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:31.920
But there's no doubt freelance&nbsp;
platforms were booming.&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:38.400
Revenue in financial year 2019 climbed to $300&nbsp;
million, then to 370 million, then to 500 million.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:46.800
By July 13, 2021, the stock had surged to $60.70.
With no end of remote work growth in sight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:51.920
despite all the anger and frustration&nbsp;
from freelancers, what could go wrong?&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.840
Quite a lot, actually. Especially&nbsp;
when you consider something else:&nbsp;

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:02.000
Upwork wasn't making any profit.
And boy, they weren't close to it either.&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.760
To attempt to get there, Upwork was about to start&nbsp;
squeezing freelancers for everything they had.

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:21.280
While Upwork was reporting record numbers, the&nbsp;
backlash over its growing fees was still brewing.&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.520
Pandemic-driven demand was so&nbsp;
strong, it seemed that the anger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:30.480
over fees never dented growth.
But it would show up soon enough.&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.320
In the meantime, Upwork was&nbsp;
giving that anger plenty of fuel.&nbsp;

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:41.520
On April 26, 2023, Upwork seemed to listen.&nbsp;
The flat 10% commission was reintroduced.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:47.280
The 20/10/5 tiers were scrapped.
Wait, was Upwork actually listening now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:51.760
Were they cutting down on the fees?
It's a bit more complicated.

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:58.560
Before the change, a $500 project incurred&nbsp;
a 20% fee, or $100. Under the new flat 10%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:05.200
system, that same project cost just $50.
For a new freelancer, this was great news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:09.280
They kept more of what they earned.
But it doubled the fee on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:13.680
platform's best freelancers.
The 5% reward for building a long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.800
high-value client relationship was gone.
Under the new system, freelancers who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.880
already crossed the $10,000 threshold were&nbsp;
pushed back to a flat 10%, instead of 5%.&nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:29.040
So, it punished Upwork's most&nbsp;
important customer: their loyal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:36.160
freelancers, who, needless to say, felt cheated.
Their cost to Upwork literally doubled overnight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:42.720
because Upwork was feeling the pressure.
After reporting a loss of $89.9 million in 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:48.160
Upwork was keen to "significantly accelerate&nbsp;
[their] progress toward long-term profitability".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:53.920
They were cutting brand spend, streamlining the&nbsp;
business, and of course, trying to increase fees.&nbsp;

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:59.600
They concluded that "the bulk of money made on the&nbsp;
platform is made by the top rated, expert-vetted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:05.680
freelancers and agencies, and it may not be most&nbsp;
profitable for Upwork to charge a lower service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.240
fee to these high-tier freelancers"
But it gets worse.&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:17.200
They added a $4.95 fee which was charged every&nbsp;
time a new contract was created. It did not matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:24.160
whether the job was a $50 gig or a $500 gig.
Though this was from the client's side.&nbsp;

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:30.000
But we're not even done.
In 2025, Upwork changed the rules again.&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.560
The flat 10% fee was gone.
Yes! Again!&nbsp;

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.320
But what replaced it was&nbsp;
possibly the worst of all.&nbsp;

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:43.680
A variable fee ranging from 0% to&nbsp;
15%, set by a hidden algorithm.&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.960
Essentially, Upwork replaced&nbsp;
a predictable commission with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:51.360
dynamic pricing. The fee now depended&nbsp;
on factors like supply and demand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:55.520
competition in your skill category,&nbsp;
and overall market conditions.&nbsp;

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:59.840
The fee also wasn't even shown&nbsp;
until you submitted a proposal.&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:05.120
That's right. You didn't even know how much you&nbsp;
were paying, or what your profit margins were.&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.840
Not only is it greedy, but the lack of&nbsp;
transparency is extremely confusing.&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.640
And once assigned, that percentage&nbsp;
was locked in for the entire contract.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:20.000
(From post): "Upwork claims this will "balance"&nbsp;
the marketplace by tweaking fees based on supply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.880
demand, complexity, and a bunch of&nbsp;
vague factors. Translation? If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.840
work in a competitive category, expect to&nbsp;
be taxed for the privilege of existing."&nbsp;

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:33.280
Two people doing similar jobs could&nbsp;
still be charged different percentages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:36.240
depending on how the algorithm classifies them.&nbsp;

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:40.240
This also made recommending Upwork&nbsp;
difficult, because no one really knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:44.080
how much they'd be making.
And it gets weirder.&nbsp;

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:51.200
High-demand skills may even qualify for 0%. Sounds&nbsp;
great, but very few would actually get this.&nbsp;

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:54.960
And of course, it was Upwork's&nbsp;
black box deciding all this.&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:00.960
On the outside this was advertised as "The&nbsp;
0% commission" as high-demand skills may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.920
qualify for a 0% commission rate.
While it advertises 0% rates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:11.680
most freelancers still pay around&nbsp;
10–15%, often without knowing why.&nbsp;

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:15.920
Just imagine trying to manage your&nbsp;
work, clients, and make any profit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:21.040
with a platform making all of these changes.
Or imagine seeing one of these announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:27.120
changes and thinking "Oh great! Fewer fees"&nbsp;
then realising it's actually MORE fees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:31.200
but just for you in particular.
The frustration which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:36.960
simmering was now at a boiling point.
But, that wasn't the only problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:50.480
Upwork had another issue, and unfortunately,&nbsp;
this one was baked in from the beginning.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:54.080
If you've watched this far,&nbsp;
you might like our videos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:58.960
We do a lot of deep dives into tech&nbsp;
companies, so consider subscribing!

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:03.520
If we look at Upwork's revenue, we can&nbsp;
see great growth during the pandemic.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:06.640
Almost $700 million in 2023.&nbsp;

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.520
But as we all know, the remote&nbsp;
work boom didn't really last.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:17.040
In Q1 2026, revenue grew year over year by… 1%.&nbsp;

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:22.480
That's not a decline, but just 1%&nbsp;
growth signals a mature market.&nbsp;

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:28.320
Stagnation, maturity, and soon: decline.
The growth stage was over.&nbsp;

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:34.160
So on May 7, 2026, Upwork announced a&nbsp;
restructuring plan that included cutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:41.040
its workforce by approximately&nbsp;
24%, around 145 jobs out of 600.&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:48.480
About a quarter of the whole company! Yikes.
But something else was happening. Something worse.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:53.600
Upwork was one of few platforms in the&nbsp;
freelance world. But after the pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:57.920
boom, that was different.
After remote work exploded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:03.920
more and more platforms began to pop up.
Many with better, more transparent pricing.&nbsp;

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.800
Some more niche for specific&nbsp;
industries. So, many freelancers left.&nbsp;

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:15.200
Or at least, they stopped signing up to Upwork.
In 2018 Upwork was "currently taking advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:19.920
the lack of competition in this market."
It wasn't the best place to find work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:25.360
but it was one of the only places.
But, Upwork was now worse than ever.&nbsp;

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:29.440
"All of my Upwork career I've been&nbsp;
cautious about getting a bad review&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:35.200
because of Upwork's horrendous appeal&nbsp;
system. Then they increased fees. And now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:39.360
I can't even talk to a potential client who&nbsp;
has literally shown INTEREST in my services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:44.400
by clicking on my profile and inviting me."
"Over the past few months, I submitted proposals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:49.760
to around 150 jobs. I also saved many of those&nbsp;
projects in my favourites so I could check them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:55.680
later. After checking them again, I found that&nbsp;
only around 30 jobs actually hired someone."&nbsp;

00:13:55.680 --> 00:14:00.000
"Upwork proposals are not&nbsp;
even getting viewed in 2026."&nbsp;

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.040
All of which made all the&nbsp;
new platforms more appealing.&nbsp;

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:06.480
And AI is only making things worse.&nbsp;

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:12.560
Since ChatGPT's launch, freelance writing job&nbsp;
postings have fallen by roughly 30%. On Upwork,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:16.480
writing projects were down&nbsp;
32% year-over-year in 2025.&nbsp;

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:22.240
The entry-level work that once formed the backbone&nbsp;
of the platform has been gutted, and with all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:28.160
the weird fees weighing on freelancers, it's&nbsp;
understandable to see why Upwork might be dying.

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:34.320
The outlook for Upwork isn't great.
Market cap is near an all-time low.&nbsp;

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:36.880
Its price-to-earnings ratio sits around 9,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:40.560
suggesting investors aren't&nbsp;
expecting much growth from here.&nbsp;

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:47.120
They are profitable now, but their GAAP net&nbsp;
income is already down 17% year over year.&nbsp;

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:52.880
So even after all that squeezing, future&nbsp;
prospects aren't looking too good.&nbsp;

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:57.280
Inside the platform, active clients&nbsp;
have been declining since 2025.&nbsp;

00:14:57.280 --> 00:15:02.160
They've annoyed all their core customers,&nbsp;
and with their current pricing, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:08.720
they'll slowly decline as people move to better&nbsp;
platforms. And that's probably for the best.

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:13.840
They're not the only ones though. Fiverr has&nbsp;
experienced a similar decline, though theirs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:19.280
is far more dramatic, and weirder. Scandal&nbsp;
after scandal, huge debt chasing growth in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:30.720
the pandemic, and a weird obsession with AI.
